1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,498
You have the slides, the handouts, and a
folder of notes.

2
00:00:03,748 --> 00:00:07,972
You reread them before the exam, and a
week later most of it is gone.

3
00:00:08,222 --> 00:00:12,153
Being tested on something does more for
memory than reading it again.

4
00:00:12,753 --> 00:00:18,175
MDlearn turns your class files, or just a
topic, into better notes you can study.

5
00:00:18,425 --> 00:00:22,470
An AI you already use writes them, a
strict compiler checks them,

6
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:26,892
and spaced repetition brings each idea
back just before you'd forget it.

7
00:00:27,342 --> 00:00:32,218
It runs on your computer, with no account.
Your notes are plain Markdown files.

8
00:00:32,818 --> 00:00:37,412
Say what you want to learn, and add any
PDFs, slides or Word files.

9
00:00:37,962 --> 00:00:40,792
MDlearn shows what it could read from each
one.

10
00:00:41,342 --> 00:00:42,948
Choose the kind of course you want,

11
00:00:43,398 --> 00:00:47,293
and who writes it: Claude from here, your
coding assistant, or a chat.

12
00:00:48,143 --> 00:00:49,917
The notes appear as they are written.

13
00:00:51,017 --> 00:00:55,562
Under the hood, every note is Markdown,
with blocks for questions and plots.

14
00:00:55,812 --> 00:01:00,307
The compiler checks every block. A broken
question is locked, never shown.

15
00:01:00,557 --> 00:01:05,892
One core runs the app, the command line,
and an MCP server for AI assistants.

16
00:01:06,142 --> 00:01:10,803
Your answers feed an FSRS scheduler, kept
in one file on your disk.

17
00:01:11,403 --> 00:01:14,270
Formulas are explained term by term, in
colour.

18
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:18,697
Plots have sliders, and derivations play
one step at a time.

19
00:01:19,747 --> 00:01:22,989
When you study, you answer, then say
whether you were sure,

20
00:01:22,989 --> 00:01:24,857
so lucky guesses come back sooner.

21
00:01:25,707 --> 00:01:27,954
Today plans a short session for each day.

22
00:01:29,154 --> 00:01:31,861
Make it yours: pick the reading font and
size,

23
00:01:32,311 --> 00:01:34,595
and highlight in colour, right in the
text.

24
00:01:35,245 --> 00:01:39,029
Select a passage, and an AI explains it
beside the text.

25
00:01:40,279 --> 00:01:42,270
And Pixel, your study companion,

26
00:01:42,270 --> 00:01:46,004
grows as you study and answers your
questions, with sources.

27
00:01:47,804 --> 00:01:52,131
Download the desktop app, or run npx
mdlearn studio.

28
00:01:52,381 --> 00:01:55,296
It's open source, under the MIT licence.

29
00:01:55,296 --> 00:01:59,378
Find it on GitHub; issues and pull
requests are welcome.
